"Energy-Driven Steering: Reducing False Refusals in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.08646v1)\nciteturn2809view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2806view0\",\"lineno\":146}); Total lines: 650\nL134: The core of our data collection is a context-aware classification of the LLM’s behavior. We define a heuristic-based classifier, $C(X,Y)$, that evaluates the appropriateness of the response $Y$ given the nature of the prompt $X$. This results in a label $l$ indicating whether the behavior is desirable (Compliant) or undesirable (Refusal).\nL135: \nL136:  | $$C(X,Y)\\rightarrow l\\in\\{\\text{Compliant},\\text{Refusal}\\}$$  |  | (4)\nL137: Specifically, the classification follows a nuanced logic: compliant responses to benign prompts are desirable, but so are refusals to harmful prompts. Conversely, refusals to benign prompts (false refusals) are undesirable, as are compliant responses to harmful prompts (jailbreaks).\nL138: Concurrently, for each generated token $y_{t}\\in Y$, we extract and store the corresponding hidden state $h_{t}$ from one or more layers of the LLM. This process populates two distinct sets of hidden states based on the classification outcome:\nL139:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{good}}=\\{h_{t}\\mid\\exists(X,Y)\\text{ s.t. }$  | $\\displaystyle((X\\text{ benign}\\land C(X,Y)=\\text{\"Compliant\"})$  |\nL140:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\lor(X\\text{ harmful}\\land C(X,Y)=\\text{\"Refusal\"}))\\land h_{t}\\text{ is from }Y\\}$  |  | (5)\nL141:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{bad}}=\\{h_{t}\\mid\\exists(X,Y)\\text{ s.t. }$  | $\\displaystyle((X\\text{ benign}\\land C(X,Y)=\\text{\"Refusal\"})$  |\nL142:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\lor(X\\text{ harmful}\\land C(X,Y)=\\text{\"Compliant\"}))\\land h_{t}\\text{ is from }Y\\}$  |  | (6)\nL143: The set $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{bad}}$ contains hidden states from contextually inappropriate trajectories (i.e., false refusals to benign prompts and compliant responses to harmful prompts), while $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{good}}$ contains states from contextually appropriate trajectories (i.e., helpful responses to benign prompts and refusals to harmful prompts). This context-aware data separation is crucial for training an EBM that can distinguish between justified and unjustified refusals.\nL144: ### 4.2 Phase 2: EBM Training\nL145: #### Energy-Based Model Formulation.\nL146: \nL147: Central to our approach is the concept of an Energy-Based Model (EBM), which is characterized by an energy function $E_{\\theta}:\\mathcal{H}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ that maps a hidden state $h\\in\\mathcal{H}=\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ to a scalar energy value. A full theoretical treatment is provided in Section cite40†C . We implement this function as a deep multi-layer perceptron (MLP) with the general form:\nL148:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{z}_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=f_{i}(\\mathbf{z}_{i-1})\\quad\\text{for }i=1,\\ldots,L\\quad(\\text{with }\\mathbf{z}_{0}=h)$  |  | (7)\nL149:  | $\\displaystyle E_{\\theta}(h)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{W}_{L+1}\\mathbf{z}_{L}+b_{L+1}$  |  | (8)\nL150: \nL151: where each function $f_{i}$ represents a layer transformation (e.g., linear projection, activation, normalization). This architecture creates a conceptual “landscape” over the LLM’s hidden state space.\nL152: #### Training Objective.\nL153: \nL154: The EBM is trained to shape this energy landscape using the InfoNCE contrastive loss, separating the states collected in Phase 1. The objective is to assign high energy to “bad” states from $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{bad}}$ and low energy to “good” states from $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{good}}$. For an anchor state $h^{+}\\in\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{good}}$ and a set of $N$ negative samples $\\{h^{-}_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N}\\subset\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{bad}}$, the loss is:\nL155:  | $$\\mathcal{L}(\\theta)=-\\log\\frac{\\exp(-E_{\\theta}(h^{+})/\\tau)}{\\exp(-E_{\\theta}(h^{+})/\\tau)+\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\exp(-E_{\\theta}(h^{-}_{i})/\\tau)}$$  |  | (9)\nL156: \nL157: Here, $\\tau$ is a temperature hyperparameter. Minimizing this loss forces $E_{\\theta}(h_{\\text{good}})\\ll E_{\\theta}(h_{\\text{bad}})$, effectively creating a classifier that can distinguish between desirable and undesirable trajectories. A formal proof is provided in Lemma cite81†C.1 .\nL158: #### Multi-Layer EBM Training Strategy.\nL159: \nL160: Our approach trains individual EBMs for multiple layers of the LLM simultaneously. For each target layer $l\\in\\{0,1,\\ldots,L-1\\}$, we train a separate EBM, $E_{\\theta_{l}}(h_{l})$, where $h_{l}$ are the hidden states from that layer. Each model $E_{\\theta_{l}}$ is trained independently using the same InfoNCE objective. After training, we evaluate each EBM’s performance on a validation set and select the best-performing models for intervention during inference.\nL161: ### 4.3 Phase 3: Real-time Gradient-Based Steering\nL162: \nL163: The final phase of our methodology involves integrating the trained EBMs into the LLM’s inference process to actively steer its generative trajectory. This is achieved through a real-time, gradient-based intervention on the model’s hidden states.\nL164: #### Steering Mechanism.\nL165: \nL166: The modification function $M(h_{t})$ introduced in our objective is realized via gradient descent on the energy surface defined by a trained EBM. For each selected intervention layer $l$, the hidden state $h_{t}^{(l)}$ is updated as follows:\nL167: \nL168:  | $$h_{t}^{\\prime(l)}=h_{t}^{(l)}-\\eta\\cdot\\nabla_{h}E_{\\theta_{l}}(h)|_{h=h_{t}^{(l)}}$$  |  | (10)\nL169: where $\\eta$ is the steering coefficient, a hyperparameter that controls the strength of the intervention. The term $\\nabla_{h}E_{\\theta_{l}}(h)$ is the gradient of the energy function with respect to the hidden state, which points in the direction of the steepest ascent on the energy landscape. By moving the hidden state in the negative gradient direction, we are performing a single step of gradient descent to find a state with lower energy.\nL170: This update rule is formally proven to minimize energy in Theorem cite82†C.1 .\nL171: #### Impact on Generation.\nL172: \nL173: The modification of the hidden state $h_{t}^{\\prime(l)}$ has a direct and immediate impact on the LLM’s output. The original probability distribution over the vocabulary is computed from the original hidden state $h_{t}^{(l)}$ (Equation cite83†2 ). After steering, the modified hidden state $h_{t}^{\\prime(l)}$ is passed to the language modeling head, resulting in a new, steered probability distribution:\nL174:  | $$p^{\\prime}_{\\text{steered}}(y_{t+1}|Y_{<t},X;\\phi)=\\text{softmax}(W_{LM}h_{t}^{\\prime(l)})$$  |  | (11)\nL175: \nL176: Let $\\Delta h_{t}^{(l)}=h_{t}^{\\prime(l)}-h_{t}^{(l)}=-\\eta\\nabla_{h}E_{\\theta_{l}}$. The change in the logits (the input to the softmax function) can be approximated by a first-order Taylor expansion:\nL177: \nL178:  | $$\\text{Logits}^{\\prime}\\approx\\text{Logits}+W_{LM}\\Delta h_{t}^{(l)}=W_{LM}h_{t}^{(l)}-\\eta W_{LM}\\nabla_{h}E_{\\theta_{l}}$$  |  | (12)\nL179: This equation explicitly shows how the steering process adjusts the logits, effectively up-weighting tokens that are more likely to lead to contextually appropriate (low-energy) continuations, and down-weighting tokens associated with contextually inappropriate (high-energy) paths.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCREST-Search: Comprehensive Red-teaming for Evaluating Safety Threats in Large Language Models Powered by Web Search (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09689v1)\nciteturn2809view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2806view1\",\"lineno\":120}); Total lines: 386\nL114: Many LLM service providers (e.g., OpenAI, Anthropic) (cite66†Longpre et al., 2024 ; cite67†Feffer et al., 2024 ) have adopted red-teaming as an essential step before releasing LLM-based products to the public.\nL115: Researchers have proposed several approaches to red-teaming LLM-based systems (cite56†Ganguli et al., 2022 ; cite55†Perez et al., 2022 ). For instance, Ge et.al (cite58†Ge et al., 2023 ) propose an iterative framework, which leverages an adversarial LLM to generate evolving jailbreak prompts. Those prompts, paired with corrected safe responses, are further used to fine-tune the target LLM, achieving scalable and automated safety enhancement for LLMs. An et.\nL116: al (cite57†An et al., 2025 ) conduct a systematic analysis towards the safety of RAG for LLMs, demonstrating that even with safe models and safe corpora, RAG can introduce new unsafe behaviors.\nL117: Despite recent progress, most red-teaming efforts have concentrated on detecting toxicity in LLM outputs under closed-text settings. In such scenarios, models operate exclusively on their internal knowledge or on information retrieved from fixed knowledge bases through RAG. These approaches overlook the additional and compounding risks that arise when LLMs are coupled with open-ended, real-time web search, where the exposure to uncurated and dynamic content substantially broadens the attack surface.\nL118: cite68†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1. Overview of CREST-Search, consisting of three main phases. (1) Adversarial search queries generation: it generates the adversarial search queries based on various harmful content categories and strategies; (2) Web search execution and risk evaluation: it executes the search query and evaluates the toxicity of the cited webpages; (3) Adversarial search queries refinement: it optimizes the query based on the judgment or terminates the round.\nL119: ## 3. Preliminary\nL120: ### 3.1. Problem Statement\nL121: \nL122: Equipped with the functionality of web search, LLMs can retrieve external information from the Internet in real time to enhance the reliability of the generated answers. We formalize the retrieval and answer generation process as below. We denote the target LLM as $M$. Given a user query $q$, the LLM first conducts a retrieval operation:\nL123: \nL124: (1)  |  | $$R(q)\\rightarrow D=\\{d_{1},d_{2},...,d_{k}\\}$$  |\nL125: where $D$ is a set of $k$ webpages or document snippets returned by the search engine. The LLM then conditions on both the original query and retrieved evidence to generate the final response:\nL126: \nL127: (2)  |  | $$M(q,D)\\rightarrow r$$  |\nL128: \nL129: where $r$ is the LLM’s answer, containing references from $D$.\nL130: \nL131: According to Luo et al. (cite54†Luo et al., 2025 ), safety risks associated with search-enabled LLMs can be categorized into the following three levels:\nL132: \nL133:   * $\\bullet$\nL134: Response risk. The response generated by the target LLM contains harmful content, such as harassment or other forms of toxicity.\nL135: \nL136:   * $\\bullet$\nL137: \nL138: Citation risk. The LLM cites external web links that contain harmful content, including the modalities of text or images.\nL139: \nL140:   * $\\bullet$\nL141: \nL142: Combined risk. Both the LLM response and cited webpages include harmful content, compounding the overall severity.\nL143: As the response risk is the target of existing red-teaming solutions for standalone LLMs, our testing objectives mainly focus on the citation risk and combined risk, which are unique in the search scenario, arising during the retrieval and citation process.\nL144: A red-teaming methodology should satisfy three core requirements. (1) It must reliably expose real safety failures across diverse target models and deployment settings. (2) It should produce a broad and representative set of test cases that cover as many harmful content categories and attack modalities as possible. (3) The generation process must be efficient and cost-effective, imposing minimal computational, time, and financial overhead to enable practical, large-scale evaluation.\nL145: ### 3.2. Threat Model\nL146: We adopt a standard threat model in which a tester seeks to assess the security of an LLM system augmented with web search.\nL147: Such red-teaming may be performed under several practical scenarios: (1) a model developer conducts internal testing to identify and remediate vulnerabilities prior to public release; (2) a prospective customer evaluates competing LLM services to select the one that best meets their safety and functional requirements; or (3) a trusted third party is engaged to perform audits and issue safety certifications.\nL148: In all cases, the tester’s objective is to simulate realistic adversarial conditions against the complete search-based LLM pipeline, revealing end-to-end failures that could arise in real-world operation.\nL149: To ensure broad applicability, we adopt a black-box setting, where the tester has no internal knowledge of the target LLM system, including (1) the model architecture and parameters; (2) the retrieval and search-integration mechanisms; (3) any deployed safety filters or mitigation strategies. The tester’s interaction is therefore limited to externally observable behavior: he may only craft and submit prompts to the system and inspect the returned responses.\nL150: Under this constraint, all evaluations must be performed using input–output probing alone, which reflects realistic auditing scenarios faced by developers, customers, and third-party auditors. We further discuss the ethical considerations of conducting such adversarial testing in Appendix cite28†A.1 , ensuring that our evaluation remains responsible and compliant.\nL151: ## 4. Methodology\nL152: \nL153: To comprehensively evaluate the safety of LLMs with web search, we present our red-teaming approach, CREST-Search, as shown in Figure cite69†1 . It adopts a three-stage pipeline to automatically generate effective test queries against the target system (Section cite15†4.1 ). We further introduce a novel adversarial search query generation strategy (Section cite16†4.2 ) and red-teaming model construction approach (Section cite17†4.3 ), to enhance the quality of the generated queries.\nL154: ### 4.1. Red-teaming Pipeline\nL155: \nL156: CREST-Search introduces an execution pipeline, to iteratively refine and produce effective testing queries against a given search-enabled LLM. This pipeline comprises three main stages: adversarial search query generation, web search execution and risk evaluation, and adversarial search query refinement\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCREST-Search: Comprehensive Red-teaming for Evaluating Safety Threats in Large Language Models Powered by Web Search (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09689v1)\nciteturn2809view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2806view1\",\"lineno\":300}); Total lines: 386\nL290:   * Feffer et al. (2024) Michael Feffer, Anusha Sinha, Wesley H Deng, Zachary C Lipton, and Hoda Heidari. 2024. Red-teaming for generative AI: Silver bullet or security theater?. In Proceedings of the AAAI/ACM Conference on AI, Ethics, and Society, Vol. 7. 421–437.\nL291:   * Ganguli et al.\nL292: (2022) Deep Ganguli, Liane Lovitt, Jackson Kernion, Amanda Askell, Yuntao Bai, Saurav Kadavath, Ben Mann, Ethan Perez, Nicholas Schiefer, Kamal Ndousse, Andy Jones, Sam Bowman, Anna Chen, Tom Conerly, Nova DasSarma, Dawn Drain, Nelson Elhage, Sheer El-Showk, Stanislav Fort, Zac Hatfield-Dodds, Tom Henighan, Danny Hernandez, Tristan Hume, Josh Jacobson, Scott Johnston, Shauna Kravec, Catherine Olsson, Sam Ringer, Eli Tran-Johnson, Dario Amodei, Tom Brown, Nicholas Joseph, Sam McCandlish, Chris Olah, Jared Kaplan, and Jack Clark.\nL293: 2022. Red Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned. arXiv:2209.07858 [cs.CL] cite88†https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.07858 L294:   * Ge et al. (2023) Suyu Ge, Chunting Zhou, Rui Hou, Madian Khabsa, Yi-Ch